Move a panic frame with one store

A frame carried its position inline -- function, file, line, column --
so moving it took four stores, and generated code moves it before every
operation that can panic. That is not a cold path: an integer add, an
array index and a call each carry a span, so the four stores were in the
body of every loop that could overflow or index out of bounds.

The position is a compile-time constant, so it becomes one: `PanicSite`
is emitted per distinct source position and the frame holds a pointer to
it. `PanicCallFrame` is `{previous, site}`, entering a frame takes the
site rather than four fields, and the trace snapshot copies pointers
rather than structs -- a site is a constant in the module, which outlives
the frame that pointed at it.

Fidelity is unchanged, which is the point of doing it this way rather
than by updating the frame less often. Every span-carrying operation
still sets its own position, so `err_out_of_bounds` still reports
`at bounds (Data/Array.tl:68:9)` and
`test_native_panic_frames_match_the_optimized_backend` still matches its
four-frame trace line for line.

Sites and the C strings inside them are interned. A function with twenty
panic sites named itself twenty times before this.

Measured, against the previous commit:

                     before     after
    array-read loop   207ms     154ms      stores in main 139 -> 100
    2M allocations     27ms      27ms
    integer loop       37ms      35ms

